# ArduPilot Rover GUIDED-mode speed-controller tuning for this tracked
# vehicle (~38-40kg total: 32.6kg base_link + 2x1.5kg tracks + attachments).
# Applied as a SECOND defaults file (see gazebo_tracked_vehicle.launch.py's
# defaults:=rover.parm,dds_udp.parm,tracked_vehicle_speed_tuning.parm)
# rather than editing the vendored ardupilot/Tools/autotest/default_params/
# rover.parm directly, since that file is shared ArduPilot upstream content,
# not project-specific.
#
# Real root cause this addresses: ArduPilot's stock rover.parm defaults
# (CRUISE_SPEED 5, CRUISE_THROTTLE 30) assume a fast, light reference rover.
# For this heavy tracked vehicle at the low speeds actually commanded
# throughout this project (0.3-0.5 m/s), the resulting feedforward throttle
# is tiny (desired_speed/CRUISE_SPEED*CRUISE_THROTTLE) -- live-confirmed
# ArduPilot's own reported velocity (/ap/twist/filtered) stayed at only
# ~0.03-0.1 m/s regardless of a much larger commanded setpoint, while the
# SAME setpoint applied directly to the Gazebo physics (bypassing ArduPilot
# entirely) achieved the full commanded speed -- proving the shortfall is
# inside ArduPilot's own GUIDED velocity control loop, not gz-sim physics.
#
# Values below start from general tracked-rover tuning guidance (heavy
# platform, high track drag/friction, especially when turning) --
# CRUISE_SPEED/CRUISE_THROTTLE lowered+raised respectively to match this
# vehicle's real low-speed operating regime, ATC_SPEED_P/I set to the
# tracked-vehicle-appropriate lower/damped range (higher P risks track
# slap/jerking on a high-inertia vehicle), ATC_SPEED_D forced to 0 (track
# noise destroys D-gain), MOT_SLEWRATE left non-zero to protect the
# (simulated) drivetrain from instantaneous throttle jumps. NOT YET
# empirically re-tuned against this vehicle's own real speed response --
# these are informed starting values, verify live before trusting them
# for anything beyond this project's typical 0.3-0.5 m/s commands.
CRUISE_SPEED    1.5
CRUISE_THROTTLE 50
ATC_SPEED_P     0.12
ATC_SPEED_I     0.10
ATC_SPEED_D     0.0
MOT_SLEWRATE    100
# GCS_PID_MASK bit 2 (value 2) streams live PID_TUNING (PID_TUNING_ACCZ
# axis) MAVLink messages for the throttle/speed loop -- target vs actual,
# straight from AR_AttitudeControl::get_throttle_speed_pid_info()
# (Rover/GCS_MAVLink_Rover.cpp). Needed to diagnose the real ~10-13%-of-
# commanded speed shortfall: is ArduPilot's own target already tiny
# (navigation layer throttling it down before the PID loop), or is target
# correctly large but the PID loop itself lacks authority to reach it.
GCS_PID_MASK    2

# REAL ROOT CAUSE of the ~10-13%-of-commanded speed shortfall above
# (found via GCS_PID_MASK's own live telemetry, see this file's git
# history): it was never a throttle/speed-PID tuning problem at all --
# ArduPilot's own internal PID target (not the throttle output) was
# already wrong, small, and NEGATIVE for a positive commanded speed.
# Traced to a ~107deg internal heading error (ArduPilot's own EKF yaw
# ~96deg vs real ground-truth yaw ~-11deg, live-measured): this vehicle
# has no GPS/navsat SENSOR wired up, but ArduPilot SITL's own simulated
# GPS (SIM_GPS1_TYPE, driven by the FDM's ground-truth position) IS
# already active by default (confirmed live: "EKF3 IMU0/IMU1 is using
# GPS" in the console log) -- it just was never being used for YAW, only
# position/velocity, so the EKF's yaw estimate was still pure gyro
# dead-reckoning with nothing to correct it, drifting unboundedly (same
# root problem as this project's earlier-documented "ArduPilot heading
# drift" bug, which only patched the ROS<->Gazebo bridge's OWN rotation
# math, not ArduPilot's internal EKF state).
#
# Fixed with EK3_SRC1_YAW=2 (AP_NavEKF_Source::SourceYaw::GPS) -- the
# standard, well-supported ArduPilot mechanism for exactly this
# no-compass-but-has-GPS scenario: EKF3 derives yaw from the GPS
# velocity vector's course-over-ground instead of gyro-only
# dead-reckoning. Requires the vehicle to be moving with enough speed
# for GPS course-over-ground to be meaningful -- expect yaw to still be
# gyro-only (and potentially wrong) for the first moment(s) after
# arming/starting from rest, converging once real motion begins.
EK3_SRC1_YAW    2
